data lease_owns_the_critical_section_note: String = "THE DEPLOY ENTRY OWNS THE LEASE; GITHUB ACTIONS DOES NOT (operator ruling 2026-08-04). Putting `flock` in the workflow would make the CI path temporarily special: the operator entrypoint would stay outside the lock, and the split between 'the deploy CI runs' and 'the deploy an operator runs' would harden into two deployment systems. So the lock is realized here, inside the live-deploy model, and every public mutation entry routes through it.\\n\\nWHY THE LOCK MUST WRAP THE WHOLE PROCESS RATHER THAN THE MUTATION. The blue/green operation is one transaction — observe route, select active/idle, prepare idle, prove readiness and readback, flip route, verify the routed result, persist the receipt, retire the old runtime. The lock must be held BEFORE the route observation, because acquiring it only around mutation leaves the original observe-then-mutate race intact: two callers can both conclude the same slot is idle. It must still be held after the flip, because releasing at the flip lets another caller observe a deployment whose receipt is not yet recorded.\\n\\nWHY NOT AN Acquire/Release EFFECT PAIR. Each live-deploy mutation, readiness probe and readback is realized as its OWN host-effect invocation. A file descriptor taken by one of those shell processes is gone when that process exits, so a modeled AcquireLease effect would return success while holding nothing across the calls that follow — a lock that type-checks and excludes nobody. Process-grain is the first realization that is honestly exclusive, because flock(2) holds across exec and releases exactly on process exit."

data lease_advisory_obligation_note: String = "flock(2) is ADVISORY, so this excludes only callers that take the same lock on the same path. That makes 'every public mutation entry routes through the leased launcher' a load-bearing obligation rather than a convention — one official entrypoint bypassing it makes the lease advisory in the bad sense.\\n\\nWHAT EXECUTES, STATED EXACTLY, because an earlier revision of this row promised more than it delivered. `test.claim.ci_deploy_witness` asserts that the function CI names IS the launcher and is distinct from both inner leased entries, that the launcher's argv is `flock --exclusive` over this target's lease path, that the conflict code and the bare-integer timeout reach that argv, and that the candidate crosses as `--arg candidate=<sha>`. Those red if the wiring is removed while every pure-decision witness stays green — which is the property that was missing when this carrier had no consumer at all.\\n\\nWHAT DOES **NOT** EXECUTE, named rather than implied: there is no planted-direct-call RED. The earlier revision of this note claimed one, and it did not exist — asserting it would mean a witness that rewrites the CI spec to name an inner entry and checks that the roster witness reds, which is a mutation of the very authority the witness reads. The honest rung here is therefore MECHANICALLY PREVENTABLE for the enrolled entrypoints and NOT structural: the inner `_leased_wet` entries stay callable by anything that invokes them directly, and no check in this repository stops that. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: reference-level visibility (the meta-exec confinement milestone), which makes the inner entries unreachable from outside this module by construction — not another runtime check, which would concede the bad state is writable."

data lease_wait_bound_note: String = "THE WAIT MUST FIT INSIDE THE JOB, AND AN EARLIER REVISION OF THIS ROW DID NOT. It set 600s and claimed 'the job backstop is 10 minutes of its own on top', which is false: the job timeout covers checkout, artifact download, unpack and the deploy together, so by the time flock starts, less than 600s remain. GitHub would cancel the job before the typed lock-conflict outcome could be returned — converting the one signal --conflict-exit-code exists to preserve into an infrastructure-shaped kill (operator review 2026-08-04).\\n\\nSo the two budgets are now ONE derivation instead of two independent literals. The cutover envelope is the measured wall of a complete blue/green attempt; the wait admits at most one such cutover running ahead of this one; and gunbc.ci_workflow's deploy step and job backstop are computed FROM these, so the containment holds by construction rather than by a comment claiming it. Changing the envelope moves the caps with it; a wait that no longer fits is unwritable rather than merely wrong.\\n\\nENVELOPE BASIS: observed deploy job windows run 3m30s-4m40s end to end (srv1-deploy-serialization-and-false-greens.md §9.3), so 5 minutes is a real ceiling rather than a target. A conflict outliving the wait is a stuck holder — a defect to diagnose, not headroom to grant."

data live_deploy_cutover_envelope_seconds: Duration = 300

data live_deploy_lease_wait_seconds: Duration = live_deploy_cutover_envelope_seconds

fn live_deploy_seconds_to_minutes_ceiling(seconds: Duration) -> Int {
  (seconds + 59) / 60
}

fn live_deploy_lease_wait_minutes() -> Int {
  live_deploy_seconds_to_minutes_ceiling(seconds: live_deploy_lease_wait_seconds)
}

fn live_deploy_cutover_envelope_minutes() -> Int {
  live_deploy_seconds_to_minutes_ceiling(seconds: live_deploy_cutover_envelope_seconds)
}

data live_deploy_step_budget_note: String = "The deploy STEP must contain a full wait followed by a full cutover, because the worst admissible case is arriving behind one holder and then doing the whole job: wait out their cutover, then run yours. Anything smaller makes the step cap, not the lease, decide the outcome."

fn live_deploy_step_budget_minutes() -> Int {
  live_deploy_lease_wait_minutes() + live_deploy_cutover_envelope_minutes()
}

type LiveDeployExclusiveInvocation {
  target: DeploymentTarget
  lock_path: FilePath
  wait_bound: FlockWaitBound
}

fn live_deploy_exclusive_invocation(target: DeploymentTarget) -> LiveDeployExclusiveInvocation {
  LiveDeployExclusiveInvocation {
    target: target,
    lock_path: deploy_target_lease_path(target: target),
    wait_bound: FlockWaitSeconds { seconds: live_deploy_lease_wait_seconds },
  }
}

data candidate_release_note: String = "THE CANDIDATE IS AN EXPLICIT VALUE, not 'whatever tree happens to be current'. It names the revision this invocation is trying to make live, and it crosses the lease's process boundary as a named argument so the inner reconciliation receives it as a parameter rather than re-deriving it. Re-deriving inside would reintroduce the defect the global tree-sync selector already demonstrated: the thing that decides which source is deployed must not be a piece of ambient host state that another concurrent run can rewrite."

type CandidateRelease {
  revision: CommitSha
}

data leased_argv_note: String = "The wrapped command is a second `gunbc run` against the SAME source roots and the same released binary, invoking the inner reconciliation entry under the lock. The candidate revision rides as `--arg candidate=<sha>`, which is the interpreter's named-argument channel rather than an environment variable — an env channel at a runtime-present site is the shell-to-intent class DESIGN names, and it would also make the candidate ambient exactly where this model is trying to make it explicit."

data live_deploy_apply_entry_path: String = "dag/gunbc/live_deploy/apply.dag"

data live_deploy_candidate_arg_name: NonEmptyStr = "candidate"

fn live_deploy_leased_command(
  invocation: LiveDeployExclusiveInvocation,
  source_roots: List<String>,
  inner_function: String,
  candidate: CandidateRelease,
) -> ArgvCommand {
  flock_exclusive_command(
    lock_path: invocation.lock_path,
    wait: invocation.wait_bound,
    command: concat(
      [release_bin_rel_path(bin_name: gunbc_bin_name)],
      gunbc_run_transport_argv_with_named_args(
        source_roots: source_roots,
        entry: live_deploy_apply_entry_path,
        function: inner_function,
        claim_run: false,
        named_args: [
          GunbcRunNamedArg {
            name: live_deploy_candidate_arg_name,
            value: candidate.revision as String,
          }
        ],
      )
    ),
  )
}

data lease_outcome_note: String = "THREE OUTCOMES, AND THE CONFLICT IS NOT A FAILURE OF THIS DEPLOY. LeaseConflict means another holder owns the target for longer than the bound — the host is busy, this run mutated nothing, and the cause is separable from the wrapped command's own exit precisely because flock reports it on a distinct code. LeaseAcquiredExit carries the inner reconciliation's own exit through unchanged, so a Keep exits zero and a Refuse exits nonzero exactly as the inner entry decided; the lease adds no verdict of its own to a run that got the lock.\\n\\nThe conflict arm is a REFUSAL rather than a retry-in-place. Retrying here would re-enter the same wait with no new information and no counter, which is the absorbing shape DESIGN §5 forbids — the frequency of lease contention has to stay observable, because a rising rate is the signal that deploys have started overlapping again."

data wrapped_exit_domain_is_closed_note: String = "THE WRAPPED PROGRAM'S EXIT DOMAIN IS CLOSED, SO CLASSIFICATION IS A WHITELIST AND NOT A PARTIAL BLACKLIST (operator ruling 2026-08-05). The wrapped command is `gunbc run`, whose exit space through std.process ProcessExit is exactly {0,1}. An earlier revision classified 111 as contention and 64..78 as launcher failure and let EVERYTHING ELSE fall through to 'the wrapped program ran' — so 126 (cannot execute), 127 (not found) and 128+N (killed by signal N) were reported as the inner reconciliation's own verdict. 'Inner reconciliation exit 127' is a sentence about a deploy that never started.\\n\\nBecause the domain is closed, the safe direction is to enumerate what the wrapped program can produce and treat everything else as abnormal. LeaseExecutionAbnormal may later split launcher failure, command-not-found and signal termination once the execution transport exposes those distinctly; today it at least stops them being attributed to the deployment decision. The sysexits arm is kept ahead of it because flock documents that range as its own, which is a stronger statement than 'not one of ours'."

type LiveDeployLeaseOutcome
  = LeaseConflict { target_key: String, waited: Duration }
  | LeaseLauncherFailed { target_key: String, exit_code: Int }
  | LeaseExecutionAbnormal { target_key: String, exit_code: Int }
  | LeaseAcquiredExit { exit_code: Int }

fn live_deploy_lease_outcome(
  invocation: LiveDeployExclusiveInvocation,
  exit_code: Int,
) -> LiveDeployLeaseOutcome {
  if flock_exit_is_lock_conflict(exit_code: exit_code) {
    LeaseConflict {
      target_key: deploy_target_key(target: invocation.target),
      waited: match invocation.wait_bound {
        FlockWaitSeconds { seconds: s } => s
        FlockFailImmediately => 0
      },
    }
  } else {
    if flock_exit_is_launcher_failure(exit_code: exit_code) {
      LeaseLauncherFailed {
        target_key: deploy_target_key(target: invocation.target),
        exit_code: exit_code,
      }
    } else {
      if wrapped_program_exit_domain_admits(exit_code: exit_code) {
        LeaseAcquiredExit { exit_code: exit_code }
      } else {
        LeaseExecutionAbnormal {
          target_key: deploy_target_key(target: invocation.target),
          exit_code: exit_code,
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

data wrapped_program_exit_domain_note: String = "std.process ProcessExit renders ExitSuccess as 0 and every exit_failure as 1, so `gunbc run` cannot produce any other code. This function is the single place that states that closure, so widening it is a deliberate edit rather than a silent consequence of adding an arm somewhere else."

fn wrapped_program_exit_domain_admits(exit_code: Int) -> Bool {
  exit_code == 0 || exit_code == 1
}

fn live_deploy_lease_conflict_reason(outcome: LiveDeployLeaseOutcome) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match outcome {
    LeaseConflict { target_key: k, waited: w } =>
      join(
        [
          "deploy refused: another deployment holds the exclusive lease on target ",
          k,
          " after waiting ",
          to_string(w),
          "s — this run mutated nothing"
        ],
        ""
      ) as NonEmptyStr
    LeaseLauncherFailed { target_key: k, exit_code: code } =>
      join(
        [
          "deploy refused on target ",
          k,
          ": ",
          flock_launcher_failure_reason(exit_code: code),
          " — this run mutated nothing"
        ],
        ""
      ) as NonEmptyStr
    LeaseExecutionAbnormal { target_key: k, exit_code: code } =>
      join(
        [
          "deploy refused on target ",
          k,
          ": the leased command exited ",
          to_string(code),
          ", which is outside the wrapped program's closed exit domain of 0 or 1 — the deploy's own verdict was never produced, so this is an execution fault (cannot-execute, not-found, or death by signal) and not a deployment result"
        ],
        ""
      ) as NonEmptyStr
    LeaseAcquiredExit { exit_code: _ } =>
      "deploy lease was acquired; this is not a conflict" as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data lease_launcher_is_the_public_entry_note: String = "THE LAUNCHER IS WHAT CI AND THE OPERATOR CALL, AND IT IS THE ONLY THING THEY CALL. Both public deploy entries in gunbc.live_deploy.apply are launchers; neither performs a deployment effect itself. That is how the advisory-lock obligation above is actually discharged — not by hoping callers remember the lock, but by the two named public entries both being this.\\n\\nWHY THE CANDIDATE IS OBSERVED HERE AND NOT INSIDE. This process holds the checkout that is performing the deploy, so HEAD here IS the revision being offered. Observing it once and passing it across the lease boundary as a named argument means the launcher and the leased reconciliation cannot disagree about which revision this run is deploying — the exact disagreement the globally-mutable tree-sync selector produced on this host, one layer up.\\n\\nHEAD IS OBSERVED BEFORE THE LOCK IS TAKEN, deliberately. It is a read of this process's own immutable checkout, not of the shared host, so nothing another deploy does can change the answer while we wait — and taking the lock first would hold it across a read that has nothing to do with the host."

data live_deploy_apply_srv1_leased_entry_function: String = "live_deploy_apply_srv1_leased_wet"

data live_deploy_retract_srv1_leased_entry_function: String = "live_deploy_retract_srv1_leased_wet"

type LeaseLaunchCandidate
  = LaunchCandidateObserved { revision: CommitSha }
  | LaunchCandidateRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }

fn observe_launch_candidate() -> LeaseLaunchCandidate {
  let head = git.Inspect.HeadCommit(expect: ExpectSuccess)
  if !head.success {
    LaunchCandidateRefused {
      cause: "deploy refused: git.Inspect.HeadCommit could not observe the candidate revision of this checkout" as NonEmptyStr
    }
  } else {
    match sha1_hex_digest(hex: head.sha as String) {
      Absent =>
        LaunchCandidateRefused {
          cause: "deploy refused: git.Inspect.HeadCommit returned a value that is not a 40-digit lowercase-hex commit sha" as NonEmptyStr
        }
      Present { value: _ } => LaunchCandidateObserved { revision: head.sha as String as CommitSha }
    }
  }
}

data lease_launch_exit_note: String = "THE LEASE ADDS NO VERDICT OF ITS OWN TO A RUN THAT GOT THE LOCK. LeaseAcquiredExit carries the inner reconciliation's exit through unchanged, so a Keep still exits zero and a Refuse still exits nonzero exactly as the leased decision made them — the launcher is a scheduler, not a second adjudicator.\\n\\nA CONFLICT IS A REFUSAL AND NOT A RETRY. Re-entering the wait would consume the same bound again with no new information and no counter, which is the absorbing shape DESIGN §5 forbids: the frequency of lease contention has to stay observable, because a rising rate is the signal that deploys have started overlapping again."

fn live_deploy_lease_launch_exit(
  invocation: LiveDeployExclusiveInvocation,
  exit_code: Int,
) -> ProcessExit {
  match live_deploy_lease_outcome(invocation: invocation, exit_code: exit_code) {
    LeaseConflict { target_key: k, waited: w } =>
      exit_failure(
        reason: live_deploy_lease_conflict_reason(
          outcome: LeaseConflict { target_key: k, waited: w }
        ) as String
      )
    LeaseLauncherFailed { target_key: k, exit_code: code } =>
      exit_failure(
        reason: live_deploy_lease_conflict_reason(
          outcome: LeaseLauncherFailed { target_key: k, exit_code: code }
        ) as String
      )
    LeaseExecutionAbnormal { target_key: k, exit_code: code } =>
      exit_failure(
        reason: live_deploy_lease_conflict_reason(
          outcome: LeaseExecutionAbnormal { target_key: k, exit_code: code }
        ) as String
      )
    LeaseAcquiredExit { exit_code: code } =>
      if code == 0 {
        ExitSuccess
      } else {
        exit_failure(reason: live_deploy_leased_run_failed_reason(exit_code: code))
      }
  }
}

fn live_deploy_leased_run_failed_reason(exit_code: Int) -> String {
  join(
    [
      "deploy failed under the exclusive lease (inner reconciliation exit ",
      to_string(exit_code),
      "); the lease was acquired, so this is this deploy's own verdict and not contention"
    ],
    ""
  )
}

data lease_coordination_subject_note: String = "THE ADMISSION IS DERIVED FROM OBSERVED FACTS, NOT FROM A TRANSPORT NAME. This assembles the coordination subject for one launch: where the lock would be taken (the command transport this launcher would use), where the mutations would land (the host-effect transport the inner reconciliation would drive), how long the lock survives, how long the cutover needs it, and which mutators the lease covers.\\n\\nAn earlier revision of this file answered the same question with `match transport { LocalShell => admit, SshShell => refuse }`. That produced the right verdict for the wrong reason, and the reason is what a future author inherits: under a name-match, admitting a future remote-session transport is a one-token edit that ALSO re-admits today's discrete-command SSH realization. Under this shape, admitting it requires reporting SessionLifetime — a claim about behaviour that the discrete-command realization cannot truthfully make (operator ruling 2026-08-05)."

fn live_deploy_coordination_subject(
  target: DeploymentTarget,
  lease_transport: CommandTransport,
  mutation_transport: HostEffectTransport,
) -> DeployCoordinationSubject {
  DeployCoordinationSubject {
    target: target,
    lease_locus: command_transport_lease_locus(transport: lease_transport),
    mutation_locus: host_effect_mutation_locus(transport: mutation_transport),
    lease_extent: command_transport_lease_extent(transport: lease_transport),
    required_extent: deploy_required_extent,
    mutation_population: deploy_mutation_population(),
    covered_population: live_deploy_leased_participants(),
  }
}

data leased_participants_note: String = "WHAT THIS LEASE ACTUALLY COVERS, AT DECLARATION IDENTITY. Every public mutation entrypoint for this target launches under this lease — apply and retract alike — and the route flip and revision record execute inside the leased apply process, so each named declaration is covered. Listing PARTICIPANTS rather than kinds is what makes a fifth entrypoint performing an existing kind visible: under the kind grain a new route-flipping function found MutateRouteFlip already covered and the admission stayed green (participant_grain_note carries the ruling). This list is what goes red when the population names a declaration the launcher does not reach."

fn live_deploy_leased_participants() -> List<DeployMutator> {
  deploy_mutation_population()
}

data lease_launch_operation_note: String = "APPLY AND RETRACT ARE TWO ARMS OF ONE LEASED OPERATION, because they mutate the same slot, the same units, the same files, and the same route-adjacent domain. Leasing only apply left retract able to race a cutover while the apply process believed it held exclusive ownership — and the carrier note claimed 'every public mutation entry routes through the leased launcher' the whole time it was false (operator ruling 2026-08-05).\\n\\nThe candidate rides on the Apply arm only, because it is the thing apply decides about and retract has no candidate to compare. Making it a field of the operation rather than a parameter of the launcher is what keeps a retract from being launched with a meaningless candidate argument."

type LiveDeployExclusiveOperation
  = LeasedApply
  | LeasedRetract

data operation_resolution_note: String = "THE CANDIDATE IS RESOLVED HERE, NOT CARRIED IN BY THE CALLER, because this process is the one holding the checkout whose HEAD the candidate IS. An earlier revision made it a field of LeasedApply, which forced every entrypoint to hand in a value it did not have — and the placeholder that resulted would have crossed the lease boundary as a real argument.\\n\\nRetract resolves to no arguments, and that is a fact about retract rather than an omission: it has no candidate to compare, because it is not deciding whether to advance."

type ResolvedLeaseOperation
  = LeaseOperationResolved { named_args: List<GunbcRunNamedArg> }
  | LeaseOperationRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }

fn resolve_lease_operation(operation: LiveDeployExclusiveOperation) -> ResolvedLeaseOperation {
  match operation {
    LeasedRetract => LeaseOperationResolved { named_args: [] }
    LeasedApply =>
      match observe_launch_candidate() {
        LaunchCandidateRefused { cause: why } => LeaseOperationRefused { cause: why }
        LaunchCandidateObserved { revision: rev } =>
          LeaseOperationResolved {
            named_args: [
              GunbcRunNamedArg {
                name: live_deploy_candidate_arg_name,
                value: rev as String,
              }
            ]
          }
      }
  }
}

fn live_deploy_leased_command_for_operation(
  invocation: LiveDeployExclusiveInvocation,
  source_roots: List<String>,
  inner_function: String,
  named_args: List<GunbcRunNamedArg>,
) -> ArgvCommand {
  flock_exclusive_command(
    lock_path: invocation.lock_path,
    wait: invocation.wait_bound,
    command: concat(
      [release_bin_rel_path(bin_name: gunbc_bin_name)],
      gunbc_run_transport_argv_with_named_args(
        source_roots: source_roots,
        entry: live_deploy_apply_entry_path,
        function: inner_function,
        claim_run: false,
        named_args: named_args,
      )
    ),
  )
}

data lease_namespace_note: String = "THE LOCK OBJECT HAS TO EXIST BEFORE flock CAN OPEN IT, AND NOTHING CREATED IT (operator ruling 2026-08-05). The launcher runs flock BEFORE entering any inner process, so a missing lock object could not be provisioned by the deploy it gates: the first run would fail at the lock, and the deploy principal cannot write under /var/lib/gunbc unaided — which this PR's own receipt writer already establishes by staging through mktemp and placing with the granted sudo install.\\n\\nSo the object is provisioned as its own step, before acquisition, through the same granted install. It is NAMESPACE PROVISIONING and deliberately not part of deployment mutation: it creates the object the coordination mechanism lives on and touches nothing the deploy decides about. `install -d` is idempotent and safe under concurrent execution — two racing provisioners converge on the same directory — which is what makes it admissible ahead of the lock rather than needing the lock it is creating. It also creates the parent, so one command establishes both.\\n\\nMODE 0755, ROOT-OWNED, AND NEITHER PRINCIPAL NEEDS WRITE ANYWHERE (operator ruling 2026-08-05). An earlier revision used 0777, because a lock FILE has to be created by whichever principal acquires first. A directory lock object is created once here, and flock(1) opens a directory read-only, so the acquiring principals need only traverse and read — which 0755 already grants. The world-writable namespace is deleted rather than argued down, and with it the replacement attack lock_object_is_a_directory_note records: a parent nobody but root can write is a parent in which the lock object cannot be unlinked or renamed."

fn live_deploy_lease_namespace_command(invocation: LiveDeployExclusiveInvocation) -> ArgvCommand {
  ArgvCommand {
    argv: [
      "sudo", "-n", "install", "-d", "-m", "0755",
      invocation.lock_path as String,
    ]
  }
}

func live_deploy_launch_under_lease(
  inner_function: String,
  operation: LiveDeployExclusiveOperation,
  mutation_transport: HostEffectTransport,
) -> ProcessExit
  uses net: Network
{
  let lease_transport = LocalExec
  let subject = live_deploy_coordination_subject(
    target: Srv1Dashboard,
    lease_transport: lease_transport,
    mutation_transport: mutation_transport,
  )
  match deploy_coordination_admission(subject: subject) {
    CoordinationRefused { cause: why } =>
      exit_failure(reason: coordination_refusal_reason(target: Srv1Dashboard, cause: why) as String)
    CoordinationCovers =>
      match resolve_lease_operation(operation: operation) {
        LeaseOperationRefused { cause: why } => exit_failure(reason: why as String)
        LeaseOperationResolved { named_args: args } => {
          let invocation = live_deploy_exclusive_invocation(target: Srv1Dashboard)
          let namespace = run_shell_command_exit_code(
            command: live_deploy_lease_namespace_command(invocation: invocation),
            transport: lease_transport,
          )
          if namespace != 0 {
            exit_failure(reason: live_deploy_lease_namespace_refusal(exit_code: namespace))
          } else {
            live_deploy_lease_launch_exit(
              invocation: invocation,
              exit_code: run_shell_command_exit_code(
                command: live_deploy_leased_command_for_operation(
                  invocation: invocation,
                  source_roots: witness_layer_roots,
                  inner_function: inner_function,
                  named_args: args,
                ),
                transport: lease_transport,
              ),
            )
          }
        }
      }
  }
}

fn live_deploy_lease_namespace_refusal(exit_code: Int) -> String {
  join(
    [
      "deploy refused: could not provision the lease namespace ",
      deploy_target_lease_dir as String,
      " (install exit ",
      to_string(exit_code),
      "); the lock cannot be acquired, so nothing was mutated"
    ],
    ""
  )
}

data lease_contention_probe_note: String = "THE WET PRECONDITION (operator ruling 2026-08-05): prove on the real host, as the real principal, that the EXACT lock path this deploy uses can be opened, exclusively acquired, contended, and released. Everything else about the lease is typechecked and hermetically witnessed, which establishes that the argv is right and the outcome partition is total — and establishes nothing whatsoever about whether flock can actually take that lock on srv1. A lease that cannot be acquired is not a safety mechanism, it is a deploy that reds at the lock.\\n\\nTHREE STEPS, AND THE FIRST ONE IS WHAT MAKES THE SECOND READABLE. `flock --nonblock` conflicts on the SAME exit code whether the conflict came from the probe's own nested attempt or from an unrelated holder, so step B alone cannot distinguish 'contention works' from 'somebody else is deploying'. Step A establishes the lock is free before B claims to have contended with itself, and step C establishes it is free again afterwards — which is the release half, and the one a lock file left behind by a crashed holder would fail if flock(2)'s release semantics were not what the citation says.\\n\\nWHY --nonblock THROUGHOUT: a probe that waits converts a genuinely-held lock into a timeout, which reports as the probe hanging rather than as the host being busy. The probe wants an answer, not the lock.\\n\\nIT USES THE REAL COMMAND BUILDER. A hand-spelled flock line here would prove that SOME flock invocation works on srv1 while the deploy's own argv stayed unexercised — the specification-without-execution shape one level over, since the thing under test is precisely the command this model constructs.\\n\\nPROTOCOL RECEIPT, executed 2026-08-05 against util-linux flock on a writable scratch path: A=0, B=111, C=0 — the nested attempt in step B opens a new file description and therefore conflicts, and the outer invocation propagates the wrapped command's exit, so the modeled conflict code is what surfaces. That receipt validates the EXIT-CODE REASONING; it does not and cannot stand in for running this on srv1, where the open question is whether the deploy principal can provision and open the production lock path at all. This probe answers that question and is the thing that has to be run there."

fn live_deploy_lease_probe_command(invocation: LiveDeployExclusiveInvocation, inner: List<String>) -> ArgvCommand {
  flock_exclusive_command(
    lock_path: invocation.lock_path,
    wait: FlockFailImmediately,
    command: inner,
  )
}

type LeaseProbeOutcome
  = LeaseProbeHolds
  | LeaseProbeRefused { cause: NonEmptyStr }

fn lease_probe_step_refusal(step: String, expected: Int, observed: Int, lock: String) -> LeaseProbeOutcome {
  LeaseProbeRefused {
    cause: join(
      [
        "lease probe failed at step ", step,
        " on lock ", lock,
        ": expected exit ", to_string(expected),
        ", observed ", to_string(observed)
      ],
      ""
    ) as NonEmptyStr
  }
}

data lease_probe_steps_note: String = "STEP A — acquire the free lock and run `true`, expecting 0: proves the object is openable and exclusively acquirable by THIS principal, which is the half that fails when the namespace does not exist or is not reachable. STEP B — hold it and, inside, attempt the same lock again, expecting the modeled conflict code: proves exclusion is real and reports on the code the outcome partition depends on. STEP C — acquire it again, expecting 0: proves the lock released when step B's process exited, which is the property that makes an expiry unnecessary.\\n\\nSTEP B WRITES A MARKER BEFORE ATTEMPTING THE NESTED LOCK, AND THE MARKER IS LOAD-BEARING (operator ruling 2026-08-05). Without it the sequence 0/111/0 can be produced by an UNRELATED holder: if some other deploy acquires the lock between A and B, B's OUTER flock returns the conflict code without ever running the nested attempt, and if that holder exits before C then C returns 0 — a passing probe that established nothing about self-contention. So B's inner command creates the marker AFTER the outer lock is held and BEFORE the nested attempt runs, and the outcome requires both the marker and the conflict code. An outer conflict now fails the probe instead of impersonating an inner one.\\n\\nSTEP D — the lock object is a directory and its parent is not writable by this principal. That is the property that makes the object's identity stable: a replaceable pathname passes A/B/C perfectly while providing no exclusion at all, because a new entrant recreates the path and locks a different inode (lock_object_is_a_directory_note carries the measured reproduction). Stated at its real rung: this proves THIS principal cannot unlink or rename the object, not that no principal can — root still can, and no probe run as an unprivileged account can establish otherwise."

func live_deploy_lease_contention_probe_wet() -> ProcessExit
  uses net: Network
{
  let invocation = live_deploy_exclusive_invocation(target: Srv1Dashboard)
  let lock = invocation.lock_path as String
  let marker = lease_probe_marker_path(invocation: invocation)
  let namespace = run_shell_command_exit_code(
    command: live_deploy_lease_namespace_command(invocation: invocation),
    transport: LocalExec,
  )
  if namespace != 0 {
    exit_failure(reason: live_deploy_lease_namespace_refusal(exit_code: namespace))
  } else {
    let cleared = run_shell_command_exit_code(
      command: ArgvCommand { argv: ["rm", "-f", marker] },
      transport: LocalExec,
    )
    let free_before = run_shell_command_exit_code(
      command: live_deploy_lease_probe_command(invocation: invocation, inner: ["true"]),
      transport: LocalExec,
    )
    let contended = run_shell_command_exit_code(
      command: live_deploy_lease_self_contention_command(invocation: invocation, marker: marker),
      transport: LocalExec,
    )
    let inner_ran = run_shell_command_exit_code(
      command: ArgvCommand { argv: ["test", "-e", marker] },
      transport: LocalExec,
    )
    let free_after = run_shell_command_exit_code(
      command: live_deploy_lease_probe_command(invocation: invocation, inner: ["true"]),
      transport: LocalExec,
    )
    let object_stable = run_shell_command_exit_code(
      command: lease_probe_object_stability_command(invocation: invocation),
      transport: LocalExec,
    )
    match lease_probe_outcome(
      lock: lock,
      cleared: cleared,
      free_before: free_before,
      contended: contended,
      inner_ran: inner_ran,
      free_after: free_after,
      object_stable: object_stable,
    ) {
      LeaseProbeHolds => ExitSuccess
      LeaseProbeRefused { cause: why } => exit_failure(reason: why as String)
    }
  }
}


data lease_probe_marker_path_note: String = "The marker lives beside the lock object's name in the runner's own temp directory, so it needs no privilege to create or remove and cannot collide with the lock namespace this probe is testing. It is evidence of one step within one probe run, cleared at the start, never state anything else reads."

fn lease_probe_marker_path(invocation: LiveDeployExclusiveInvocation) -> String {
  join(["/tmp/gunbc-lease-probe-", deploy_target_key(target: invocation.target), ".marker"], "")
}

fn live_deploy_lease_self_contention_command(
  invocation: LiveDeployExclusiveInvocation,
  marker: String,
) -> ArgvCommand {
  let nested = shell_command_render(
    command: live_deploy_lease_probe_command(invocation: invocation, inner: ["true"])
  )
  live_deploy_lease_probe_command(
    invocation: invocation,
    inner: [
      "sh", "-c",
      join(["touch ", sh_single_quote(s: marker), "; ", nested], ""),
    ],
  )
}

fn lease_probe_object_stability_command(invocation: LiveDeployExclusiveInvocation) -> ArgvCommand {
  ArgvCommand {
    argv: [
      "sh", "-c",
      join(
        [
          "test -d ", sh_single_quote(s: invocation.lock_path as String),
          " && test ! -w ", sh_single_quote(s: deploy_target_lease_dir as String),
        ],
        ""
      ),
    ]
  }
}

fn lease_probe_outcome(
  lock: String,
  cleared: Int,
  free_before: Int,
  contended: Int,
  inner_ran: Int,
  free_after: Int,
  object_stable: Int,
) -> LeaseProbeOutcome {
  if cleared != 0 {
    lease_probe_step_refusal(step: "0 (clear the self-contention marker)", expected: 0, observed: cleared, lock: lock)
  } else if free_before != 0 {
    lease_probe_step_refusal(step: "A (acquire a free lock)", expected: 0, observed: free_before, lock: lock)
  } else if inner_ran != 0 {
    lease_probe_step_refusal(
      step: "B (the outer lock must be HELD BY THIS PROBE before the nested attempt; an absent marker means the conflict came from an unrelated holder)",
      expected: 0,
      observed: inner_ran,
      lock: lock,
    )
  } else if contended != flock_conflict_exit_code {
    lease_probe_step_refusal(
      step: "B (a second attempt while held must conflict)",
      expected: flock_conflict_exit_code,
      observed: contended,
      lock: lock,
    )
  } else if free_after != 0 {
    lease_probe_step_refusal(
      step: "C (the lock must be free once the holder exits)",
      expected: 0,
      observed: free_after,
      lock: lock,
    )
  } else if object_stable != 0 {
    lease_probe_step_refusal(
      step: "D (the lock object must be a directory whose parent this principal cannot write, so its identity cannot be replaced under a live lease)",
      expected: 0,
      observed: object_stable,
      lock: lock,
    )
  } else {
    LeaseProbeHolds
  }
}
